# Xcode
#
# gitignore contributors: remember to update Global/Xcode.gitignore, Objective-C.gitignore & Swift.gitignore

## User settings
xcuserdata/

## Obj-C/Swift specific
*.hmap

## App packaging
*.ipa
*.dSYM.zip
*.dSYM

## Playgrounds
timeline.xctimeline
playground.xcworkspace

# Swift Package Manager
#
# Add this line if you want to avoid checking in source code from Swift Package Manager dependencies.
# Packages/
# Package.pins
# Package.resolved
# *.xcodeproj
#
# Xcode automatically generates this directory with a .xcworkspacedata file and xcuserdata
# hence it is not needed unless you have added a package configuration file to your project
# .swiftpm

.build/

# CocoaPods
#
# We recommend against adding the Pods directory to your .gitignore. However
# you should judge for yourself, the pros and cons are mentioned at:
# https://guides.cocoapods.org/using/using-cocoapods.html#should-i-check-the-pods-directory-into-source-control
#
# Pods/
#
# Add this line if you want to avoid checking in source code from the Xcode workspace
# *.xcworkspace

# Carthage
#
# Add this line if you want to avoid checking in source code from Carthage dependencies.
# Carthage/Checkouts

Carthage/Build/

# fastlane
#
# It is recommended to not store the screenshots in the git repo.
# Instead, use fastlane to re-generate the screenshots whenever they are needed.
# For more information about the recommended setup visit:
# https://docs.fastlane.tools/best-practices/source-control/#source-control

fastlane/report.xml
fastlane/Preview.html
fastlane/screenshots/**/*.png
fastlane/test_output

# macOS
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# Secrets — never in the tree (CLAUDE.md non-negotiable)
#
# #
# Matched at ANY depth, deliberately. A path-specific rule protects only the
# location it names, and this rule once said `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` while an
# older `Support/Secrets.xcconfig` still sat on disk from a previous design —
# one `git add -A` away from being committed. The committed template is called
# Secrets.example.xcconfig and does not match this.
Secrets.xcconfig is the one file that holds real credentials. Its
# committed template, Config/Secrets.example.xcconfig, is NOT matched by the
# pattern below and stays tracked.
#
# Matched at ANY depth, deliberately. A path-specific rule protects only the
# location it names, and this rule once said `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` while an
# older `Support/Secrets.xcconfig` still sat on disk from a previous design —
# one `git add -A` away from being committed. The committed template is called
# Secrets.example.xcconfig and does not match this.
Secrets.xcconfig
#
# .env is not part of this project's build any more — Xcode reads .xcconfig,
# not .env. The pattern stays as a safety net: a stray .env from another tool,
# or an old one still sitting in a working copy, must never become committable.
.env
.env.*
*.p8
*.p12
*.mobileprovision
*.cer

# Xcode build products
DerivedData/
build/

# Generated by `make generate` — never committed.
#
# The Xcode project is GENERATED from project.yml by XcodeGen. project.yml is the
# reviewable source of truth; a binary .pbxproj is not reviewable and two people
# working in one tree would corrupt it. Run `make generate` after a fresh clone.
ZenTomato.xcodeproj/
Support/ZenTomato-Info.plist
# The widget extension's Info.plist, generated from project.yml for exactly the
# same reason as the app's. It is a build product, not a source file.
Support/ZenTomatoActivity-Info.plist

# Build products and test results
DerivedData/
*.xcresult

# Your Canva working file. Kept out of the tree deliberately: it is ~7MB of
# embedded raster (not vector), it is a derivative of a CC BY-SA photo, and the
# brush ring inside it is stock artwork whose licence forbids redistributing it
# as a standalone file. The shipped icon is drawn from scratch instead — see
# Design/icon/make-icon.sh.
Design/ZenTomato.svg

# --- Private working documents ------------------------------------------------
#
# Research and drafts that belong to the owner rather than to the project. This
# one was swept into a commit by a `git add docs` that named a directory instead
# of the files actually changed, pushed to a branch, and had to be removed from
# that branch's history afterwards.
#
# The lesson is the pattern rather than the file: stage what you changed, not
# where you were working.
docs/dual-licensing-report.md
docs/*-report.md
#
# AND ANY ARCHIVE OR OFFICE DOCUMENT DROPPED IN docs/. The rule above was
# written the same day `git add docs` swept up a private report, and it was
# still not enough: hours later `git add -A` committed a 54KB
# "ZenTomato redesign scope.zip" that nobody had opened. A glob covering one
# file extension protects against exactly one shape of the same mistake.
#
# Working material lands in docs/ constantly. Anything that is not prose the
# project owns is opted out by default here, and a genuinely wanted file is one
# `git add -f` away.
docs/*.zip
docs/*.pdf
docs/*.docx
docs/*.pages
docs/*.key
docs/*.sketch
DerivedData-release/
